Pre-release audit (main @ 3e4e592) found dzil build ships far more than the distribution. dzil build --in /tmp/x produced a tarball whose MANIFEST includes, alongside lib/ and t/:

.claude/agents/*.md, .claude/rules/*.md, .claude/settings.json, .claude/skills/**/*.md
.karr (contains the internal orchestration prompt text verbatim)
.gitignore
TODO.md (internal German implementation-plan notes)
CLAUDE.md
docker-compose.yaml, Makefile.docker
k8s/forgejo-test/*.yaml plus Makefile
scripts/setup-forgejo-test.sh

Root cause: dist.ini only has @Author::GETTY with no PruneFiles section (and no MANIFEST.SKIP). Git::GatherDir gathers every git-tracked file; PruneCruft only strips generic build byproducts, not project-specific dirs like k8s/ or docker-compose.yaml. Net effect: every dzil release would publish internal agent prompts, karr board bootstrap text, and local test-infra manifests to public CPAN.

Suggested fix (for www-forgejo-worker): add a PruneFiles section to dist.ini excluding .claude, .karr, .gitignore, TODO.md, docker-compose.yaml, Makefile.docker, k8s, scripts (decide whether CLAUDE.md should stay public or be pruned too), or an equivalent MANIFEST.SKIP. Re-run dzil build and confirm the MANIFEST only lists lib/t/Changes/LICENSE/META files/Makefile.PL/README/cpanfile/dist.ini afterward.

Found during: www-forgejo-release-checker audit of main (commit 3e4e592). Not a code-behavior defect, packaging/dist.ini only. Blocks a clean dzil release.

2026-09-18 23:24 Scope decision from maintainer: ONLY .karr is pruned. All other originally-suggested candidates (.claude/**, .claude/settings.json, .gitignore, CLAUDE.md, docker-compose.yaml, Makefile.docker, k8s/forgejo-test/*, scripts/setup-forgejo-test.sh) stay in the tarball on purpose — do not add them to PruneFiles later without a new decision. TODO.md was already removed from the repo separately, so it needed no PruneFiles entry.

Change: dist.ini gets [PruneFiles] with 'filename = .karr', placed directly after [@Author::GETTY] (plugin bundle expands in place; an explicit plugin listed after it is appended to the plugin chain, so PruneFiles runs alongside the bundle's own PruneCruft in the prune phase — no conflict).

